         as voice_message_stop, because to her it is the same act. -->
    <string name="read_aloud_stop">■ Stop</string>
    <!-- The way back from a magnified screen. Names the result she wants, not the gesture she
         made: she may have no idea she pinched anything. -->
    <string name="zoom_reset">↺ Normal size</string>
    <!-- The way back from the full-screen photo viewer. One large, unmistakable key at the
         bottom of the screen, matching the zoom reset key. -->
    <string name="photo_viewer_close">← Back</string>
    <!-- A voice message. Says what pressing it does, not what the file is: "audio message" is
         a description of a format, "Listen" is a description of what happens. -->
    <string name="voice_message_play">▶ Listen</string>
    <string name="voice_message_stop">■ Stop</string>
    <!-- A film. "Watch" for the same reason the voice message says "Listen": it names what
         happens when she presses it. Deliberately not "Play video", which names a file and a
         verb she associates with a machine. There is no matching stop control — the film has a
         screen of its own, and that screen closes the way every other one does. -->
    <string name="video_message_play">▶ Watch</string>
    <!-- How long the film runs, under the button that starts it, so that pressing it is not a
         decision made blind. In words rather than as "1:47", which is a convention to decode
         before it is a fact — see FilmLength for why the precision is spent on that instead. -->
    <plurals name="video_message_length_seconds">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d second</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d seconds</item>
    </plurals>
    <plurals name="video_message_length_minutes">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d minute</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d minutes</item>
    </plurals>
